  "generated_text": "Given that \\( p(x) = Ax^5 + Bx^3 + Cx + 4 \\) is divided by \\( x - 3 \\) and the remainder is 11, we can use the Remainder Theorem. According to the Remainder Theorem, \\( p(3) = 11 \\).\n\nNext, we need to find the remainder when \\( p(x) \\) is divided by \\( x + 3 \\). To do this, we can use the fact that \\( p(x) \\) can be expressed as:\n\\[ p(x) = (x - 3)q(x) + r(x) \\]\nwhere \\( q(x) \\) is the quotient and \\( r(x) \\) is the remainder.\n\nWe know that \\( p(3) = 11 \\). Therefore, we can write:\n\\[ p(-3) = p(3) \\]\n\nSince \\( p(-3) \\) is equal to \\( p(3) \\), we have:\n\\[ p(-3) = 11 \\]\n\nThus, the remainder when \\( p(x) \\) is divided by \\( x + 3 \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{11} \\]",
  "grade": {
